    "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/job/First-Hill/Staff-Attorney---Tribal-Estate-Planning-and-Legal-Access-Fellowship_JR100222-1": {
      "title": "Staff Attorney - Tribal Estate Planning and Legal Access Fellowship",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-27\nPosted: Posted 9 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Onsite Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Position Description: Seattle University School of Law invites applications for a 0.5 FTE Staff/Contract Attorney to lead its new Tribal Estate Planning and Legal Access Fellowship within the Northwest Center for Indigenous Law (NCIL). This is a non-benefits-eligible, grant-funded position focused on expanding access to legal services and supporting the Fellowship’s mission. The Fellowship is designed to strengthen Seattle University School of Law's partnerships with Tribal Nations and Indigenous communities, provide students with practical training in Tribal estate planning and legal access issues, and expand access to culturally responsive legal services for Tribal citizens. During its inaugural year, the Fellowship will focus on providing estate planning services to citizens and community members affiliated with the Makah Tribe, the Confederated Tribes of the Chehalis Reservation, and the Elders Program of the Seattle Indian Health Board (SIHB). In collaboration with community partners, the Fellowship may also identify and assess additional unmet legal needs affecting Tribal communities and develop recommendations for future programming. The successful candidate will possess expertise in Federal Indian Law, Tribal law, and Indian estate planning, including the unique legal issues surrounding trust and restricted lands, probate of trust property, the American Indian Probate Reform Act (AIPRA), Tribal probate codes, Tribal wills, and the intersection of Tribal, federal, and state inheritance systems. The Fellowship will enroll its first student Fellow during the Spring 2027 semester and continue through the Summer 2027 semester. The appointment will begin Spring 2027, and conclude Summer 2028. Subject to funding, program needs, and performance, the appointment may be renewed for an additional year. Position Summary The Staff Attorney will design and supervise the Tribal Estate Planning and Legal Access Fellowship. The position combines mentorship, community engagement, and direct legal service. Working closely with NCIL faculty and staff, Tribal partners, and participating students, the Fellowship will help create a sustainable model for Tribal community-based legal services. Qualifications Required Qualifications J.D. degree from an ABA-accredited law school. Active membership in good standing with the Washington State Bar Association or eligibility for admission in Washington. Minimum of three years of legal practice experience. Demonstrated knowledge of Federal Indian Law, Tribal law, and Indigenous legal issues. Experience with estate planning, probate, trust administration, or closely related practiceareas Knowledge of Indian estate planning, including Tribal wills, trust and restricted lands, probate procedures, and the American Indian Probate Reform Act. Excellent interpersonal, and communication skills. Ability to work collaboratively with Tribal governments, Indigenous organizations, students, faculty, and community members. Preferred Qualifications Experience supervising/mentoring legal interns Experience working directly with Tribal governments, Tribal courts, or Indigenous communities. Familiarity with Washington Tribal Nations and the legal issues affecting Indigenous communities in the Pacific Northwest. Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Compensation at a Glance $50,000 Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate’s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3–6 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university’s discretion."
    },
    "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/job/First-Hill/Billodue-Makerspace-Lab-Manager_JR100236": {
      "title": "Billodue Makerspace Lab Manager",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-27\nPosted: Posted 9 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Onsite Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Position Description: 30 % -   Make rspac e   Operation s and Ov ersight   The   Billodue   Makerspace   Lab Manager   oversees the daily functioning of the space, ensuring it is approachable, well-supported, and ready for use by a wide range of students, including those with no prior experience. This includes   maintaining   tools, equipment, and materials while   establishing   clear, consistent systems that make participation straightforward. The role focuses on reducing friction through intuitive processes, transparent expectations, and right-sized safety practices, while managing supplies,   scheduling, and real-time user support. Attention to both operations and user experience helps sustain a space that invites exploration, lowers intimidation, and encourages independent engagement.   Key Responsibilities: Manage daily operations, access, and front-line user support   Maintain tools, equipment, and materials for reliability and usability   Implement clear, accessible safety and training practices   Oversee inventory,   purchasing , and supply systems   Coordinate space usage and improve ease of participation   2 5 % - Mentorship and Supervision     The   Billodue   Makerspace   Lab Manager   develops and sustains a student-centered staffing model that is essential to both the operations and culture of the space. Student staff are not only operators, but peer educators and community builders. This role includes recruiting, training, and supervising a team of student employees who support others in navigating tools, processes, and creative work. Emphasis is placed on developing students’ confidence, judgment, and ability to support peers from a wide range of backgrounds. Through structured training and ongoing mentorship, the   Billodue   Makerspace   Lab Manager   fosters a collaborative   team   culture grounded in care, shared responsibility, and continuous learning.     Key Responsibilities:   Recruit, train, and supervise student staff   Develop onboarding and ongoing training for technical and facilitation skills   Foster an inclusive, collaborative team environment   Mentor students in peer support and user engagement   Create leadership pathways for returning staff   20 % - Community Building and Outreach   The   Billodue   Makerspace   Lab Manager   contributes to a culture that   centers   belonging, creative exploration, and broad participation. This includes developing programming that invites experimentation, play, and connection, particularly for students who may not initially see themselves as “makers.” In collaboration with the Manager, the role supports relationships with student organizations and campus partners to expand engagement and ensure the space reflects a wide range of identities and creative practices. Through outreach, events, and communications, the   Billodue   Makerspace   Lab Manager   helps position the makerspace as an open, inclusive environment where students are encouraged to explore, take risks, and grow.   Key Responsibilities: Develop programs that encourage creativity, exploration, and connection   Support relationships with student groups and partners in collaboration with the Manager   Create accessible entry points for new and underrepresented users   Promote an inclusive, multidisciplinary understanding of making   Contribute to outreach and engagement efforts   20% - Academic and Institutional Support     Working in collaboration with the Manager, Library Makerspaces, the   Billodue   Makerspace   Lab Manager   supports the integration of making into teaching and learning across disciplines. This includes assisting faculty in developing makerspace-based assignments and translating course goals into hands-on activities that emphasize exploration, process, and creative inquiry. The role contributes to workshops and instructional sessions and develops materials that help students engage meaningfully with tools and creative practices. In coordination with the Manager, the role also supports partnerships with campus units that expand experiential learning and invite broader participation in making as a form of inquiry and expression.     Key Responsibilities:   Support faculty collaboration on makerspace-based assignments and projects   Translate learning goals into hands-on, student-centered activities   Design and deliver workshops or instructional sessions   Develop instructional resources and documentation   Participate in campus partnerships in coordination with the Manager     MARGINAL JOB FUNCTIONS   5% - Other Duties as Assigned   •         Remain knowledgeable and informed about trends in experiential learning and makerspace technologies   •         Attend Library/division/department meetings   •         Contribute to Library committees and working groups as assigned   •         Perform other duties as needed       WORK SCHEDULE  Monday-Friday,   10am-6pm . Makerspace events may take place outside regular working hours, requiring some   schedule   flexibility. To serve a year-round academic community, the library is   open   some legal or university holidays and staff may   be required   to work a full or partial shift on such days.   MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS    Specific Knowledge and Abilities:   Knowledge of principles, methods, and techniques related to   maker   practice and experiential   learning .   K nowledge and skills related to sewing , fashion, fabric arts,   and/ or textile design.   Knowledge and skills in a variety of craft and maker processes and technologies.   Ability to plan, organize, and schedule activities .   Ability to   operate   maker equipment and tools .     Ability to   maintain   usage   data and information .     Ability to adjust and perform simple maintenance of technical equipment .      Ability to lift, push, and pull 40 pounds.    Willingness to accept and support the mission of Seattle University and its commitment to the vision and values of Jesuit and Catholic higher education.    Ability to   work   a flexible schedule that may involve evenings and weekends as needed.   This position requires a background check   of criminal history .   PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS   Two or more years’   previous   experience working in higher education academic makerspaces, libraries, media, or technology centers.   Experience with crafting, woodworking,   or   bicycle   repair and maintenance.   Experience with   intermediate electronics (circuit boards, Arduinos/Raspberry Pi, IoT devices, etc.) .   Experience   with   the intersection of   Artificial Intelligence   and the maker movement .   Ability to deliver training and instruction to users with   different experience   levels and cultural backgrounds.   Experience leading the work of students.   Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Compensation at a Glance $62,700 – $69,400 Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate’s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3–6 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university’s discretion."
    },
    "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Lemieux-Library/Temporary-Library-Access-Services-Team-Member_JR100232": {
      "title": "Temporary Library Access Services Team Member",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-27\nPosted: Posted 9 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nMonths Per Year: 10, 15-20 hours per week Work Model: Onsite Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Position Description: Under the supervision of the Manager of Access Services, the Cornish Access Services Librarian, and the Lead Evening Technician, this position oversees library facilities and operations during weekend, morning, evening, and opening or closing shifts. Success in this patron-focused role requires strong organizational, communication, and attention-to-detail skills. The temporary staff member will staff the circulation desks at both libraries; lend materials, and help students use library collections and technology; perform Access Services functions of varying complexity; answer questions about Lemieux Library policies, procedures, services, and resources; and contribute to unit and library planning through meetings, training, workshops, and collaborative projects and initiatives. This role provides high-quality, user-centered service to students, faculty, staff, and community members. It advances the library’s commitment to https://www.seattleu.edu/strategic-directions/ and to affirming and supporting Seattle University’s Black, Indigenous, and People of Color (BIPOC) community through the https://www.seattleu.edu/office-of-diversity-and-inclusion/lift-su-our-action-plan/ . The ideal candidate will demonstrate a record of actively advancing diversity, equity, and inclusion in the workplace. Essential Job Functions with Percentage of Work for Each Listed 90% – Access Services Departmental Duties    Works closely with both Manager, Access Services, the Lead Evening Technician, and the Cornish Access Services Librarian, to ensure consistent frontline service at Lemieux Library on Friday and Saturdays as well as up to one weekday at the Cornish Library.  Securely opens and closes both Lemieux and Cornish Libraries in line with library policies, procedures, and protocols. Works regular Circulation Desk shifts at both libraries, performing simple to complex Access Services functions, and ensuring that institutional and consortial materials are processed correctly. Answers informational, directional, and service questions, utilizing equipment, and online tracking tools. Assists patrons in navigating the library website and locating library materials online and in the building. Makes appropriate referrals to other library and  campus services as needed. Contributes to student employee evaluations and any additional re-training and feedback.  Provides basic technology assistance and equipment problem solving on Fridays and Saturdays.   10% - Building Support Duties   Primary Saturday and secondary Friday contact for both Public Safety and Facilities Services for any building or security related issues. Coordinates access to library rooms and spaces for events, workshops, and classes. Secondary Saturday contact for all building-related questions, concerns or work orders for the Lemieux Library.  Marginal Job Functions Acquires and maintains cross-trained skills to provide “floating” or back-up services when and where needed in both Cornish and Lemieux libraries. Works in support of department or library service priorities, as assigned by supervisors. Performs other duties as assigned. Work Schedule This position is 15-20 hours per week over a 10-month contract that spans the three academic quarters/semesters of Fall, Winter, and Spring; September 1st to June 30th. Shifts include one weekend (Saturday), and evening closing shifts (Friday) at Lemieux Library and one weekday at Cornish Library (floating weekday). To serve a year-round academic community, the library is open for some legal or university holidays, and staff may be required to work a full or partial shift on such days. Minimum Qualifications Proof of enrolment in an accredited ITC or MLIS program and/or 1 or more years’ previous library experience, or an equivalent combination of education and work experience sufficient to successfully perform the essential job functions.  Excellent service-oriented interpersonal and communication skills with the ability to establish positive rapport with students, faculty and staff; supervisors; library colleagues; and university staff.  Demonstrated commitment to diversity, equity, and inclusion. Demonstrated commitment to student success. Ability to prioritize and meet expected deadlines, use judgment and analytical skills for problem solving, and seek consultation when appropriate or necessary.     Aptitude to work in a complex setting and to work both independently as well as collaboratively in a team environment. Competence with Microsoft Office 365 Suite.    Ability to lift, push, and pull 40 pounds.   Willingness to accept and support the mission of Seattle University and its commitment to the vision and values of Jesuit and Catholic higher education. This position requires a criminal history background check. Preferred Qualifications Two or more years’ previous experience working in academic or public libraries, in an access services/circulation role. Familiarity with Ex Libris’ Alma/Primo Integrated Library System. Familiarity with Alma Analytics, Oracle Analytics, or SQL databases. Experience working with or supporting diverse populations. Understanding of the Library of Congress classification system. Familiarity with library databases and emerging technology. Experience leading the work of students. Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Compensation at a Glance $27.90 per hour Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate’s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3–6 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university’s discretion."
    },
    "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/job/First-Hill/Executive-Coordinator_JR100229": {
      "title": "Executive Coordinator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-27\nPosted: Posted 9 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Onsite Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Position Description: Seattle University is seeking an experienced administrative professional to join the Office of the Provost. As Executive Coordinator, you will support the work of the Vice Provost for Academic Programs, Vice Provost for Academic Effectiveness, and the Academic Assembly by scheduling and staffing meetings, coordinating projects, compiling information, preparing reports and correspondence, and managing files. The ideal candidate is a dynamic professional who can support the day-to-day operations of the Academic Affairs and Academic Assembly and adapt to change in a fast-paced environment. You must handle confidential matters with professionalism, work with a positive and solutions-oriented mindset, and demonstrate strong judgment, tact, confidentiality, and diplomacy.   Support for the Vice Provosts (50%) Manage calendars, travel details, incoming external calls for the Vice Provosts, ensuring sufficient work time and time for meetings and events. Assist with emails as directed. Communicate with internal and external constituents, drafting responses to correspondence, where appropriate. Coordinate meeting and event logistics, including room reservations, A/V, technical support, and catering. Submit, track, and reconcile expenditures. Process invoices, requisitions, reimbursements, and payments for the office. Develop working knowledge of key Academic Programs processes including curriculum, program review, new program development, accreditation, academic policy, catalog and systems coordination, and other strategic initiatives. Support critical Academic Program team functions including coordination of timelines and materials to help ensure effective workflow across colleges, schools, departments, and university offices. Support accreditation and licensure activities including tracking accreditation activity deadlines, posting of accreditation results, and providing clear communication regarding accreditation and licensure standards/requirements and the current accreditation status of the university and academic programs. Work in partnership with the Provost, Senior Vice Provosts, Vice Provosts, and Deans, and their office staff on all business needs. Support for the Academic Assembly (50%) Schedule and staff meetings for the Academic Assembly and its committees (e.g., Executive Committee, Faculty Handbook Revision Committee, Academic Policy Review Committee, Faculty Welfare Committee, Committee on Committees, Bylaws Committee, and Program Review Committee). Process faculty stipends. Manage meeting and event logistics, including distribution of materials, room reservations, A/V, technical support, and catering. Prepare meeting minutes. Provide administrative support for the Academic Assembly officers. Coordinate projects related to the Academic Assembly areas of work, including academic policy, faculty handbook, faculty welfare, curriculum, and committees. Coordinate the Academic Assembly processes for nominating and appointing faculty to committees and amending the faculty handbook.   Manage the Academic Assembly’s files and SharePoint website.   Critical Success Factors Confidentiality, diplomacy, tact, discretion, and collaboration are required to work in the Provost’s Office and with Academic Assembly. Courteous and efficient handling of all contacts. Diplomacy in responding to visitors, phone calls, and customer service issues. Willingness to receive work from executive staff and senior level administrators, often with little or no instructions or established practices or procedures. Comfort exercising independence and making judicious decisions based on thorough knowledge of functional area policies and procedures. Minimum Qualifications Bachelor’s degree required. Five years of experience providing successful high level administrative support. Strong and positive customer service and support experience within a highly visible business office, including phone, written, and personal contact. Experience with efficiency in a high-volume office with frequent interruptions. Demonstrated ability to multi-task and prioritize projects. Strong attention to detail, organizational and planning skills. Experience with event planning. Excellent verbal and written communication skills required including experience preparing, reviewing, and editing correspondence and documents. Advanced working knowledge of all Microsoft Office products. Quickly learn new systems and adapt to existing practices, while also looking for efficiencies in existing processes. Demonstrated reliability, professional discretion, and confidentiality. Demonstrated can do, positive attitude and willingness to pitch in as needed.   Preferred Qualifications Experience supporting a senior executive and working with faculty. Knowledge and experience in higher education. Understanding of a university’s organization and operations. Experience with project management. Knowledge of Robert’s Rules of Order. Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Compensation at a Glance $58,000 - $62,000 Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate’s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3–6 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university’s discretion."
    },
    "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Seattle-University/Kinesiology-Program-Manager_JR100154": {
      "title": "Kinesiology Program Manager",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-27\nPosted: Posted 9 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Onsite Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Position Description: Laboratory Learning Coordination; Human Performance Lab and Mobile Lab (50%) Maintain, update, and produce laboratory operating policies and procedures reflecting industry standards and best practices Ensure that laboratory facilities and users are compliant with requisite operational policies and procedures (including, but not limited to health and safety regulations/guidelines for employees, students, and research participants; and ethics and human subjects research rules) Maintain operating knowledge and quality assurance/control in Kinesiology laboratory equipment, devices, and tools (i.e., Vicon Motion Capture, K4B2, Parvo Medics TruOne, Xsens, Force Plates, Bioelectrical Impedance Analysis, Gymaware, EMG, Ultrasound) utilized for teaching, learning, and research (including collecting, analyzing, and interpreting data) Responsible for installation, maintenance, repair, management, and troubleshooting of equipment and computing software and hardware for laboratory equipment Serve as resource for operation of laboratory facilities related to instructional/course needs Responsible for hiring, onboarding, and supervising students employed for duties/responsibilities associated with the department’s laboratory facilities Organize and lead activities that facilitate collaborative research among and between Kinesiology students and faculty, internal and external stakeholders Program Operations (25%) Liaise with faculty and staff responsible for college- and university-level operations, especially the College’s Deans’ Office, Office of the Registrar and Office of Admissions, to facilitate success of academic programs and ensure department administrators, faculty, and staff are informed of current, updated academic programs related information, policies, and procedures Support the coordination of undergraduate internship processes and activities in collaboration with the Internship Coordinator, faculty, students, and external partners, including assisting with the development and maintenance of relationships with internship sites and community partners. Execute (routine and ad hoc) processes and procedures for course catalog building and modifications, in collaboration with department and college administrators/faculty Manages class schedule building processes, working closely with department and college administrators and staff (as relevant); including collaborating with faculty to arrange and revise course scheduling Assist department and college administrators with developing and implementing program review and improvement initiatives, including program assessment, evaluation, and strategic planning Assist in organizing and executing department faculty and staff retreat(s) and meetings; assist with college retreat Onboard and orient new and adjunct faculty in collaboration with college-level staff and administrators (as relevant) Manages faculty and staff professional development requests and reimbursements Assists in development of yearly departmental budgets and producing financial reports and summaries Student Recruitment and Success (20%) Coordinate and staff student recruitment and orientation events and activities (including occasionally outside of business hours) in coordination with college and university programming/initiatives Manage and maintain student records and database (including individual-level information related to personal contact, academic standing, and program progress) Monitor graduate and undergraduate student progress and retention, in partnership with academic advisors; diagnosing actual and potential student issues regarding fulfilling program requirements and compliance with academic policies and procedures Collaborate in efforts to assist students with compliance with relevant student handbooks and policies Support academic advisors and faculty advisors in guiding students with timely completion of program-related forms and currency of documentation of academic milestones and achievements As appropriate, keeps department administrator(s), faculty, department and college staff, and academic advisors appraised and updated on student progress at the individual- and cohort-level Facilitate, as needed and appropriate, communication between students and staff, faculty, or administrators at the department, college, and university levels Communications and Marketing (5%) Monitor and maintain department-related internet and intranet sites to assure accurate, up-to-date information Ensure department-related marketing and communication assets, especially information related to academic programs, are current and accessible across various forms of media and channels (digital, print, web, social media, etc.) Assist in obtaining information and content about pursuits and accomplishments of faculty, staff, students, alumni, and external partners across department programs, for college- and university-level marketing, communications, and advancement staff Maintain and provide (historic and current) information related to departmental academic programs, events, and systems QUALIFICATIONS:     Bachelor’s degree.Graduate degree (Master’s or Doctoral) in discipline/field related to Kinesiology is preferred Demonstrated administrative and operational support experience.Experience working in an academic environment is preferred Strong collaborative skills, including ability to work in team environment Ability to communicate effectively through written, oral, and interpersonal ways Ability to organize, prioritize, and perform multiple tasks and responsibilities to facilitate completion Demonstrated commitment to diversity and inclusivity in line with the university’s mission, vision and values All positions at Seattle University require a criminal history background check Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Compensation at a Glance $72,500 - $75,000 Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate’s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3–6 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university’s discretion."
    },
    "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/job/First-Hill/Instructor--Biology_JR100318-1": {
      "title": "Instructor, Biology",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-26\nPosted: Posted 10 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nPosition Description The Department of Biology at Seattle University invites applications for a full-time Instructor position for the 2026-27 academic year. This position is a temporary replacement for current faculty on sabbatical leaves. We are seeking an instructor to teach some combination of introductory courses (BIOL 1610+1611, 1620+1621, 1630+1631), majors courses including genetics, anatomy and physiology, virology, and cell biology, and/or non-majors courses on cancer biology or global health. Specific course assignments will be determined based on the candidate’s expertise and departmental needs. Ideal candidates will demonstrate expertise in a relevant field of biology and a commitment to undergraduate education and teaching excellence. Minimum requirements include a Masters degree, or equivalent, in a relevant biological field and undergraduate teaching experience. Salary Range: $55,000 - $61,000, dependent on experience Application Instructions: Submit applications to https://www.seattleu.edu/careers, and include a curriculum vitae; a cover letter that includes your future teaching plans and courses of interest; also include the names and contact information for three references. Review of applications will begin August 25, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Inquiries may be directed to Dr. Carolyn Stenbak (stenbakc@seattleu.edu). Founded in 1891, Seattle University is a Jesuit Catholic university located on a beautiful campus of more than 50 acres in the dynamic heart of Seattle. Our diverse and driven population is made up of more than 7,200 students enrolled in undergraduate and graduate programs within eight schools and colleges. Seattle University is an equal opportunity employer. In support of its pursuit of academic and scholarly excellence, Seattle University is committed to creating a diverse community of students, faculty and staff that is dedicated to the fundamental principles of equal opportunity and treatment in education and employment regardless of age, color, disability, gender identity, national origin, political ideology, race, religion, sex, sexual orientation, or veteran status. The university encourages applications from, and nominations of, individuals who will further enrich the diversity of our educational community. Compensation at a Glance: Salary Range: $55,000 - $61,000, dependent on experience. Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting.  The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate’s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck.  Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person.  Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs.  Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage.  Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% non elective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%.  You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events.  Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, and paid sick leave.  For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/"
    },
    "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/job/First-Hill/Service-Desk-Technician_JR100225": {
      "title": "Service Desk Technician",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-26\nPosted: Posted 10 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Hybrid Work Eligible Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Position Description: Essential Responsibilities Monitor incoming service channels and the ticketing system and respond to customer requests in a timely manner .   Provide support in person, over the phone, by email, and remotely.   Accurately record customer issues in the ticketing system (currently Jira).   Provide support for a variety of incidents and requests, including but not limited to print issues, shared drive access , account and network issues, software installs , device reprovisioning, etc.   M aintain and update support documentation to assist in information sharing within the team and in the external knowledge base .    Take ownership of customer issues from first contact and take accountab ility for the solution and customer experience through resolution .   Collaborate with other ITS team members to determine and provide solutions.     Marginal Job Functions   Perform other duties as assigned.   Minimum Qualifications   A demonstrated commitment to diversity, inclusion and equity, and the ability to live out the university’s mission, vision, and values.   Previous experience providing front-line technical support .   Previous experience with incident management, problem management, and knowledge base tools.   Strong understanding of ITIL best practices.   Proficiency in supporting PC/Mac computing environments and applications.   Proficiency in supporting basic A/V equipment .     Demonstrate d customer service orientation and the ability to organize, analyze, prioritize, and problem-solve within a fast-paced environment.   Ability to communicate difficult/sensitive information tactfully.   Ability to remain calm, maintain perspective , and respond in a professional manner when faced with tough situations.   Advanced analytical and technical problem-solving skills .    Self -starter with attention to detail, flexibility, and the ability to adhere to project specifications and timelines.   Demonstrated ability to produce effective, professional, and accurate written correspondence.   Personal accountability for project timelines and milestones.   Ability to work successfully individually and in a team-oriented environment.   Active listener to ensure client and team needs are fully understood and met   Ability to exercise sound judgment in complex situations.    Experience using and supporting a service management system to process and prioritize work   assignments.   All positions at Seattle University require a criminal history background check.     Preferred Qualifications   Experience working in higher education.   Experience with SharePoint issues   CompTIA A+ certificat e .   Working knowledge of computer repair and iPhone troubleshooting   Intermediate to a dvanced knowledge of MacOS support.   Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Compensation at a Glance $48,000 - $52,000 Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate’s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3–6 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university’s discretion."
